                    <msContents><!-- describe the intellectual content of an inscription -->
                        <summary>This inscription, tentatively dated to 980 C.E. (for undefined reasons) by B.L. Rice identifies a subordinate of Āhavamalla or Taila II who was son of Iṟivabeḍaṁga a receiver of the blessing of Cāmuṇḍa, and ruler of the Banavāsi 12000 and another territory of which the name has been lost. 
                            The inscription contains the beginning of an introduction to his subordinate, but cuts off there. The stone fragment on which the inscription is engraved was found north east of the Praṇavēśvara temple.</summary>
                        <!-- offers the possibility to give a summary of the inscription's content -->
                    </msContents>
